---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { emails } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="About — Ivan Minier"
  description="Ivan Minier is the founder of Tideframe Labs and a Public Communication student at the University of Vermont. He works across research, writing, design, and software."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1>I like work that finally makes sense.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I am Ivan Minier. I live in Vermont, study Public Communication at the University of Vermont,
        and founded Tideframe Labs. I work across research, writing, design, and software — and sometimes on ideas that are not software at all.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>
        The through-line is ordinary. I keep running into useful things that are exhausting in practice:
        a television catalog that looks like a spreadsheet, rental photos with no structure, a Mac that stays
        in one arrangement all day, campus housing that is hard to talk about clearly, laundry rooms you cannot see until you walk there.
      </p>
      <p>
        I try to understand those well enough to reorganize them, and to explain them clearly to the people who have to use or evaluate
        them. The finished thing should feel obvious.
      </p>

      <h2>Studying Public Communication</h2>
      <p>
        I switched from Business Administration to Public Communication at UVM, in the Department of Community Development and
        Applied Economics (CDAE), College of Agriculture and Life Sciences. The direction fits the work better: research,
        writing, and explaining systems clearly are as much a part of what I build as the code is. Trinity Redistricting is the
        clearest example — a feasibility argument that has to survive maps, numbers, and public review, not just look good as a
        rendering. The interface and documentation work across Tideframe's products leans on the same instinct.
      </p>

      <h2>What I care about</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <article class="card"><strong>Products that explain themselves</strong><p>If a person has to decode the interface, the product is still part of the mess.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><strong>Power without noise</strong><p>Software can be capable without being loud. Native platforms help with that.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><strong>Private by default</strong><p>Rental records, playlists, and workspace profiles do not need to live on somebody else’s server.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><strong>Put the right things next to each other</strong><p>Move-in photos stay attached to the room they document. A channel keeps its guide.</p></article>
      </div>

      <h2>A short project arc</h2>
      <div class="timeline">
        <article><strong>2025</strong><p>UVM LaundryLink — a campus tool so students can see laundry rooms before they walk across campus.</p></article>
        <article><strong>2026</strong><p>Trinity Redistricting — an independent housing feasibility vision for UVM’s Trinity Campus.</p></article>
        <article><strong>2026</strong><p>Tideframe Labs — Modeboard for Mac, then Tideframe Rental Record on the App Store, then Lineup in development.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/resume">Read the full résumé →</a></p>

      <h2>How to reach me</h2>
      <p>
        Write to <a href={emails.primary.href}>{emails.primary.address}</a>.
        School mail is <a href={emails.school.href}>{emails.school.address}</a>.
        Personal mail is <a href={emails.personal.href}>{emails.personal.address}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
